## CRITICAL — how to invoke agents (v22.2)

The pipeline's agent prompts live at `.cursor/agents/*.md`. When a user issues a trigger below, you **MUST**:

1. **Load the matching agent prompt file FULLY** (read the entire file, not a summary)
2. **EXECUTE it** by following its phase instructions step-by-step using real tool calls (file reads, file writes, grep, etc.)
3. **NOT respond conversationally** with a prose summary of what the agent would do — that is a failure mode

If the user types a plain trigger like `Analyze project`, treat it as equivalent to `@project-analyzer.md analyze the codebase and produce the agent's declared outputs`.

**Recommended user invocation pattern** for maximum reliability:

```
@<agent-file>.md <specific instruction>
```

Examples:
- `@project-analyzer.md Analyze project`
- `@project-analyzer.md Rescan NextJS/Components`
- `@orchestrator.md Work on {TICKET_ID}`
- `@orchestrator.md Work on https://<host>/browse/{TICKET_ID}` (URL also supported)
- `@explorer.md Run the explorer`
- `@surgeon.md Run the surgeon`
- `@review.md Run the review`
- `@ship.md Ship it`

Bundle-mode (multi-story) — separate dedicated agents. **Per-story execution is the default** (per-ticket gates between tickets, ONE consolidated PR). Pass `--linear` to opt out:
- `@bundle-orchestrator.md Work on epic stories {ID}, {ID}, {ID}, ...`
- `@bundle-orchestrator.md Work on epic stories {ID}, {ID}, {ID}, ... --linear` (consolidated execution — every-N checkpoints, one_per_task commits)
- `@bundle-explorer.md Run the bundle explorer`
- `@bundle-surgeon.md Run the bundle surgeon`
- `@bundle-review.md Run the bundle review`
- `@bundle-ship.md Ship the bundle`

Plain triggers (`Analyze project`, `Work on ...`) work via this rule, but `@<file>` is more explicit and avoids ambiguity.

## Auto-start
On `Work on {TICKET}`, `Work on story {TICKET}`, `Work on https://.../browse/{TICKET}`, or similar, start with orchestrator (Step 1). Orchestrator's load_context step 2b extracts `{TICKET_ID}` from any of these forms — see `orchestrator.md § Phase: load_context (A0) § step 2b` for the full grammar.

## Auto-start (BUNDLE MODE — multi-story consolidation)

> 🛑 **CRITICAL — DO NOT SPLIT THE BUNDLE.** When the user issues a bundle trigger (explicit list `Work on epic stories A, B, C, D, E` or status-filter form), the **only** valid response is to dispatch ALL listed tickets, **as a single bundle**, to `bundle-orchestrator.md`. Do **NOT**:
> - Suggest dividing the list into `bundle1 / bundle2 / bundleN` choices
> - Auto-partition by layer (frontend bundle, backend bundle, ...)
> - Auto-partition by parent epic when `cross_epic_policy: warn_continue` is set
> - Drop tickets you judge "too large" or "out of scope" — that's the user's call at the C.3 gate
> - Group consecutive tickets and ask "process group A first?"
>
> If the list size exceeds `runtime.bundle.max_tickets`, the **agent itself** halts during A.1 with a clear "split or override --max=" message. The OUTER LLM (this routing layer) never proposes splits — it dispatches verbatim. The user can shrink the bundle by replying `Drop <ticket>` at the orchestrator's C.3 gate. That is the ONLY split mechanism. A pre-emptive chooser destroys the consolidated requirement summary the orchestrator builds — once the user picks one of the offered sub-bundles, the synthesis context for the unchosen tickets is lost.
>
> **Single bundle in → single bundle out.** Always.

When the user issues either of these patterns, route to **bundle-orchestrator** (NOT the regular orchestrator):

| Trigger pattern | Dispatch | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `Work on epic stories <ID>, <ID>, <ID>[, ...]` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Explicit ticket list. If `parent` field disagrees across the IDs, behavior controlled by `runtime.bundle.cross_epic_policy` (default: warn + continue). |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> with status="<S1>","<S2>"[,...]` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Status-filtered child discovery. Each status must appear in `jira.status_groups` or `jira.status_map`. |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> status="<S>"` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Terser form, same as above. |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> group=<key>` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Status-group shorthand (`group=active_hydrate` etc., from `jira.status_groups`). Off when `runtime.bundle.status_filter.allow_groups: false`. |

**Disambiguation from single-story trigger:** the bundle router checks for the keyword `epic` AND either (a) two-or-more comma-separated ticket IDs after `epic stories`, or (b) the substring `with status=` / `status=` / `group=` after a single epic ID. Anything else routes to the regular orchestrator. The single-story trigger `Work on PROJ-1234` is unaffected.

**Pre-flight gates fired by `bundle-orchestrator` (in this order, before C.3):**

| Gate | When it fires | What user picks | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| BR.0 — bundle resume | `_bundle-state.yaml` already exists for the same computed `BUNDLE_ID` | `Resume` / `Resume from T<N>` / `Resume --fresh` / `Inspect state` / `Abandon bundle` | Resume / re-synth / halt |
| BR.0c — pre-existing context | ANY per-ticket file (`mode: story`/`bundle-card`) for any candidate ticket OR overlapping bundle state | `Resume <BUNDLE_ID>` / `Re-run --fresh` / `Skip implemented` / `Show <id>` / `Cancel` | Branch back to BR.0 OR set fresh flags OR proceed |
| A.1.5 — implementation evidence | After A.1 fetches tickets, BEFORE A.2 path computation | `Go` (accept SKIP/REVIEW_ONLY recommendations) / `Include all` / `Skip <ids>` / `Force <ids>` / `Show evidence <id>` / `Cancel` | Trim `{tickets}`; populate `{review_only_roster}` |
| C.3 — final gate | After Phase B writes all artifacts | `Go` / `Amend: <text>` / `Drop <ticket>` / `Show details` / `Cancel` | Branch creation (C.4) only on `Go` |

All four are `⛔ MANDATORY HALT` gates. The orchestrator must render the gate and wait for the user's reply before any subsequent step. Auto-progressing past any of these gates is a bug.

**Default execution mode: per-story.** Bundle mode runs in **per-story** execution by default (per-ticket gates, `one_per_ticket` commits, ONE PR). Set `runtime.bundle.execution_mode: consolidated` in `pipeline.yaml` to flip the project-wide default; trigger flags below override per-run.

**JIRA labels for bundle correlation (auto-applied):** Every ticket in a bundle is automatically labeled in JIRA so any single ticket clearly shows it was processed in bulk mode and which siblings it traveled with. Configurable via `runtime.bundle.jira_labels` in `pipeline.yaml`:

```yaml
runtime:
  bundle:
    jira_labels:
      enabled: true                                 # set false to disable
      prefix: "agentic_team_bulk"               # all labels start with this string
      mode: "all_tickets"                           # all_tickets | siblings | bundle_id | both
        # all_tickets (DEFAULT — matches user request format):
        #   Every ticket gets len(bundle) labels — one per bundled ticket (including itself).
        #   Example for bundle [PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432]:
        #     PROJ-1234 gets: agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234,
        #                     agentic_team_bulkPROJ-2345,
        #                     agentic_team_bulkPROJ-3432
        #     PROJ-2345 gets the same 3 labels
        #     PROJ-3432 gets the same 3 labels
        #   JIRA query "labels = agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234" returns ALL 3 tickets.
        # siblings:
        #   Every ticket gets len(bundle)-1 labels — one per OTHER ticket. Smaller label
        #   footprint; opening a ticket shows its siblings but not itself.
        # bundle_id:
        #   Every ticket gets ONE label of form: {prefix}{BUNDLE_ID}
        #   (e.g. agentic_team_bulkIIQMAG-100-BUNDLE-A3F2). Cleanest single-correlation;
        #   loses per-sibling visibility.
        # both:
        #   Combination of bundle_id + all_tickets. Most labels but maximum queryability.

      apply_at: "both"                              # orchestrator_c4 | ship_phase6 | both (default)
        # orchestrator_c4: applied right after branch creation (early visibility)
        # ship_phase6:     applied alongside JIRA transitions at end-of-bundle
        # both:            applied at C.4 AND re-applied at ship Phase 6 (idempotent;
        #                  catches cases where C.4 failed, was skipped, or labels were
        #                  manually removed mid-bundle)

      include_review_only: true                     # apply labels to {review_only_roster} tickets too
      include_skipped_by_evidence: false            # by default, do NOT label tickets that A.1.5
                                                    # marked SKIP (already-shipped tickets stay clean)

      # Idempotency: applying a label that's already present is a no-op (JIRA API). Safe to
      # re-run. Existing labels on the ticket (added by humans, other automation) are
      # preserved — labels are unioned, never overwritten.
```

**JIRA-side semantics:**
- Apply via `story_source` MCP (or `gh` / `glab` CLI fallback if vcs MCP) using the host's label-update endpoint. JIRA: `PUT /rest/api/3/issue/{key}` with `{"update":{"labels":[{"add":"<name>"}, ...]}}`.
- Labels are case-insensitive in JIRA; normalize to lowercase prefix + verbatim ticket ID.
- Failure to apply a label → record in `_bundle-state.yaml` `stages.<stage>.label_apply_failures[]` and proceed; ship Phase 6 retries non-blocking.
- Auto-applied labels are NOT removed if the bundle is cancelled or `Drop <ticket>` is used — they remain as audit trail. To remove manually: `Amend: remove jira labels` at C.3 (orchestrator-side cleanup) or via JIRA UI.

**Surfacing at gates:**
- Bundle-orchestrator C.3 gate: shows the intended label set so user sees what will be applied on `Go`.
- Bundle-ship Phase 6: shows labels alongside transitions in the per-ticket section.
- `_bundle-state.yaml` records final applied label set per ticket under `stages.ship.labels_applied[ticket][]`.

**Why this matters:** without these labels, a bundle is invisible from JIRA. Users browsing JIRA see "PROJ-1234 in In Progress" with no signal that it's part of a 5-ticket bundle being implemented together. Querying "what was bundled with PROJ-1234?" requires reading commit messages or pipeline state files. With auto-labels, a single JIRA query (`labels = agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234`) returns the whole bundle.

**Optional flags on the bundle trigger:**

| Flag | Alias | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `--deep` | — | Phase A runs grep-based file-overlap analysis across the working tree per story (~10–20K extra tokens, amortized). Default off (set by `runtime.bundle.deep_overlap_default`). |
| `--max=<N>` | — | Override `runtime.bundle.max_tickets` for this run. Cannot exceed pipeline-config max. |
| `--fresh` | — | Same semantic as single-story `--fresh`: ignore prior `_bundle-state.yaml` and re-synthesize from JIRA. The branch is NOT deleted. |
| **`--linear`** | **`--no-each`, `--consolidated`** | **Opt out of per-story execution** (per-story is the default — see above). Reverts to legacy consolidated execution: `task_ordering: layer_dep`, every-N-tasks checkpoints in surgeon/review, `one_per_task` commit grouping in ship. Use for small bundles (≤3 tickets), dense shared code, or autonomous one-shot runs where per-ticket gates feel ceremonial. Sticky across resumes — once `flags.each: false` is in state, downstream agents respect it. |
| `--each` | `--per-story` | **No-op (default behavior).** Per-story execution is the default. Kept as a backward-compat alias; emits a non-blocking "redundant flag" notice. Mutually exclusive with `--linear` (passing both halts). |
| `--offline` / `--skip <names>` / `--only <names>` | — | Same flag family as single-story; pass through to MCP role resolution. |

**Trigger directives (`context:`, `reference:`, `out_of_scope:`, `constraints:`):**

Free-form natural-language directives that carry priority guidance into orchestrator synthesis. They work on **both** single-story and bundle triggers (orchestrator.md and bundle-orchestrator.md parse them identically).

| Directive | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `context: <free text>` | Catch-all priority guidance — pattern hints, file paths to mirror, layers to include, design direction. **Additive** to the JIRA ticket scope/ACs; not a replacement. |
| `reference: <TICKET-ID>` | Pull another ticket's LLD/PR as a pattern source (existing mechanism — diff-based reuse mining at A0.6). |
| `out_of_scope: <free text>` | Anti-scope addition. Extends JIRA's §Boundaries — never contradicts, only adds. |
| `constraints: <free text>` | Performance / browser / a11y / deadline constraints. Adds to PART 1 §Constraints alongside JIRA-stated constraints. |

**Per-decision priority (directives are additive, not a flat global override):**

The most common confusion: directives do NOT outrank JIRA ACs globally. The correct framing is per-decision-type. JIRA body + ACs (from MCP or `ticket-input.md`) remain authoritative for **what** must be built; directives shape **how** it gets built and **what additional context** to consider.

| Decision type | Priority order (highest → lowest) |
|---|---|
| Scope — what to build (deliverables, ACs) | JIRA body + ACs > `context:` (additive only — can ADD a layer JIRA didn't mention; cannot REMOVE an AC JIRA stated) |
| Anti-scope — what NOT to touch | UNION (JIRA §Boundaries, `out_of_scope:`) — additive |
| Pattern / style / file paths / naming | `context:` path hints > `reference:` ticket pattern > project-map > defaults |
| Layer inclusion (FE / BE / DB / config / i18n / tests) | UNION (JIRA-implied layers, `context:` layer hints) — additive |
| Constraints (perf, browser, a11y) | UNION (JIRA-stated, `constraints:` directive) — additive |
| Reuse decisions | `reference:` ticket > `context:` path hints > project-map shared_paths > grep |
| Pass/fail verdict at bundle-review | JIRA ACs (authoritative). `user_context_compliance: PASS|PARTIAL|FAIL` is an additional advisory flag — drives PARTIAL on the bundle verdict but does not override AC pass/fail |

**Conflict resolution.** If a directive contradicts a JIRA AC text (e.g., AC says "modal", `context:` says "inline"), the orchestrator surfaces the conflict at the **A.5 checkpoint gate** as a blocking item. The user resolves via `Amend:` (refine the AC OR refine the directive) — orchestrator never picks silently because AC text is authoritative for review verdicts and the user must own the decision.

**Why this matters.** The `context:` directive is an **additional priority input layered on top of the ticket**, not a replacement for it. The ticket (from JIRA / `ticket-input.md`) defines the contract; directives shape the implementation and add cross-cutting guidance. Treating directives as an override of the ticket would let a one-line `context:` silently break the AC contract — which is exactly what the conflict-detection at A.5 prevents.

**Parsing rules:**

```
1. The directive starts with the keyword + colon at the beginning of a line
   (or after a space following the main "Work on ..." trigger).
2. Capture extends from immediately after the colon to the next recognized
   directive OR end-of-trigger, whichever comes first.
3. Newlines INSIDE a directive's value are preserved (free-form text).
4. Code fences (```) inside a directive are preserved verbatim.
5. Length cap per directive: 2000 chars (~500 tokens).
   Beyond the cap → orchestrator HALTS with:
     "{directive}: block exceeds 2000 chars. Move long-form spec into
      contexts/ticket-input.md and trigger again."
6. Unknown directive (e.g. `pattern: ...`) → HALT with the canonical
   "unknown directive" message naming the recognized list. Do NOT silently
   absorb into the previous directive — typos must be loud.
7. Directives are independent of `--flags`. Order doesn't matter; both can
   appear in the same trigger.
8. The same directive twice → HALT ("duplicate directive: context:").
   Use multi-line within a single directive instead.
```

**Examples (single-story):**

```
Work on story IIIWJKN-1234
context: Mirror the pattern in src/components/metadata/Cards/ —
         implement card + form + backend + DB. Same naming convention.

Work on PROJ-1234
reference: PROJ-100
context: Same approach as PROJ-100 but with the new audit log requirement.
out_of_scope: bulk operations — defer to a follow-up

Work on PROJ-1234 --fresh
constraints: Must work on IE11 (legacy customer); first paint <500ms; no new bundle deps.
```

**Examples (bundle):**

```
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432
context: Refer to the metadata folder pattern (cards + form + backend + DB).
         Each ticket is a different page/card design but the layered structure
         should match. Backend should reuse the existing CardController base.

Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345 --linear
reference: PROJ-77
context: Mirror PROJ-77's approach but skip the legacy fallback path.

Work on epic <EPIC_ID> with status="To Do"
context: All children share the same auth wrapper — implement once in shared,
         reuse across tickets.
out_of_scope: i18n — picked up by a separate epic
```

**Where directives are persisted:** orchestrator writes them into the contexts file frontmatter (single-story: `$CONTEXTS_FILE`; bundle: `$BUNDLE_CONTEXTS_FILE`) as `user_context`, `reference`, `out_of_scope`, `constraints`. Downstream agents read these and use them as priority input during their own per-task work. Sticky on resume — to change, re-issue the trigger with new directives (replaces) OR use `Amend: replace context with: <new text>` at the orchestrator A.5 / C.3 gate.

**Conflict resolution:** if a directive contradicts the JIRA AC text or a `reference:` ticket's pattern, the orchestrator surfaces the conflict at the **A.5 checkpoint gate** as a blocking item — user resolves via `Amend:` (refine the directive) or accepts (orchestrator picks the higher-priority source per the table above).

**Quick reference:**

```
# Default — per-story execution (no flag needed):
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432
Work on epic <EPIC_ID> with status="In Progress","To Do"

# Opt out — consolidated/legacy execution:
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432 --linear

# Combined with --fresh to re-synthesize:
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345 --fresh           # default per-story, fresh
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345 --linear --fresh  # consolidated, fresh
```

Execution shape (default — per-story):

```
bundle-orchestrator   (Go at C.3)
  └─ bundle-explorer  (Go at Phase 0; runs entire bundle's exploration in one pass)
      └─ bundle-surgeon (Go at Phase 0)
          ├─ Implement SHARED prelude tasks (multi-source) → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-1234's tasks               → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-2345's tasks               → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-3432's tasks               → end-of-stage gate
          └─ ...
              └─ bundle-review (Go at Phase 0)
                  ├─ Review SHARED tasks       → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-1234's tasks  → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-2345's tasks  → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-3432's tasks  → end-of-stage gate
                  └─ bundle-ship (Go at Phase 0)
                      → commit-per-ticket (one_per_ticket, forced)
                      → ONE PR listing all tickets
                      → JIRA transitions per ticket
```

The total number of `Go` gates under `--each` with N implementing tickets is roughly:
`5 (one start-of-stage per agent) + 2N (per-ticket gates in surgeon and review) + ~3 (orchestrator pre-flight, ship verdict, ship final)`.
That's a feature, not a bug — `--each` is for users who want explicit pause-points. Use the consolidated default if you want fewer interruptions.

**Bundle agents are DEDICATED — separate from single-story agents.** Bundle mode and single-story mode have entirely different agent files. There is no shared dispatch:

| Stage | Single-story / bug agent | Bundle agent | Trigger (bundle) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Step 1 — synthesis | `agents/orchestrator.md` | `agents/bundle-orchestrator.md` | `Work on epic stories <IDs>` / `Work on epic <ID> with status=...` |
| Step 2 — exploration | `agents/explorer.md` | `agents/bundle-explorer.md` | `Run the bundle explorer` |
| Step 3 — implementation | `agents/surgeon.md` | `agents/bundle-surgeon.md` | `Run the bundle surgeon` |
| Step 4 — review | `agents/review.md` | `agents/bundle-review.md` | `Run the bundle review` |
| Step 5 — ship | `agents/ship.md` | `agents/bundle-ship.md` | `Ship the bundle` |

**Crucial routing rule — single-story triggers NEVER route to bundle mode:**

- `Run the explorer` / `@explorer.md ...` → ALWAYS goes to `agents/explorer.md`. If that file resolves a contexts file with `mode: bundle`, it HALTS with a redirect to `@bundle-explorer.md`. It does NOT auto-route.
- `Run the surgeon` / `Run the review` / `Ship it` → same rule. Each regular agent refuses bundle context and prints a redirect.
- This guarantees single-story / bug behavior cannot be polluted by bundle logic and vice-versa. The user's trigger choice picks the agent — frontmatter only validates that the choice was correct.

**Resume triggers (bundle mode) — all route to the dedicated bundle-* agents:**

| Trigger | Dispatches to |
|---|---|
| (Re-issuing the original `Work on epic stories ...` / `Work on epic <ID> ...`) | `bundle-orchestrator` — pre-flight `detect_bundle_resume` step routes to the right resume point. |
| `Resume bundle-orchestrator for <BUNDLE_ID>` | `bundle-orchestrator` — explicit re-entry into Phase A/B if interrupted. |
| `Resume bundle-explorer for <BUNDLE_ID> from task T<N>` | `bundle-explorer.md` — reads `_bundle-state.yaml`, skips done tasks. |
| `Resume bundle-surgeon for <BUNDLE_ID> from task T<N>` | `bundle-surgeon.md`. |
| `Resume bundle-review for <BUNDLE_ID> from task T<N>` | `bundle-review.md`. |
| `Resume bundle-ship for <BUNDLE_ID>` | `bundle-ship.md` — idempotent re-run. |

If the trigger names a `<BUNDLE_ID>` that has no `_bundle-state.yaml`, the bundle agent halts with: "No bundle state file found. Verify the bundle ID or re-issue the original `Work on epic stories ...` trigger."

**Why dedicated bundle agents (architectural rationale):**

Earlier versions used mode-detection inside the regular agents — `explorer.md` checked frontmatter and lazy-loaded `modes/bundle-explorer-flow.md` when it saw `mode: bundle`. That coupling caused two regressions: (1) bundle gates leaked into single-story decision paths, and (2) "Run the explorer" was ambiguous about which mode would actually run, depending on which contexts file resolved first. Splitting into dedicated `bundle-*.md` files makes the trigger the explicit mode selector. Single-story agents stay byte-identical for non-bundle work.

**Bundle-mode flow files (loaded by the dedicated bundle agents only):**

| Bundle agent | Loads flow file |
|---|---|
| `agents/bundle-explorer.md` | `agents/modes/bundle-explorer-flow.md` |
| `agents/bundle-surgeon.md` | `agents/modes/bundle-surgeon-flow.md` |
| `agents/bundle-review.md` | `agents/modes/bundle-review-flow.md` |
| `agents/bundle-ship.md` | `agents/modes/bundle-ship-flow.md` |

`bundle-orchestrator.md` is self-contained — no separate flow file.

## Step 0 triggers (project-analyzer — one-time or on-demand rescan)

| User command | Dispatches to | Mode |
|--------------|---------------|------|
| `Analyze project` / `Scan project` / `Run project-analyzer` | project-analyzer | Full scan (creates project-map.md). Errors if file already exists → suggest `Rescan project`. |
| `Rescan project` | project-analyzer | Full rescan with diff preview |
| `Rescan frontend` / `Rescan backend` / `Rescan tests` | project-analyzer | Layer-scoped rescan |
| `Rescan <Stack>` (e.g. `Rescan Java`, `Rescan AngularJS`, `Rescan Python`) | project-analyzer | Stack-scoped rescan — stack token resolved against `skills.layer_map` keys + aliases |
| `Rescan <Stack>/<Section>` (e.g. `Rescan Java/Services`, `Rescan Python/REST`) | project-analyzer | Narrow stack + section rescan |
| `Rescan components` / `services` / `endpoints` / `REST` / `contracts` / `consumers` / `templates` / `build` / `config` / `folders` / `stack` / `promotions` | project-analyzer | Section-scoped rescan |
| `Rescan since {date}` / `Rescan since last rescan` | project-analyzer | Git-delta rescan |
| `Rescan path: {dir}` / `Rescan {folder}` (legacy) | project-analyzer | Path-scoped rescan |
| `Reconsider ignored` | project-analyzer | v18 — force-display all entries in `analyzer_ignore` for review, regardless of growth threshold |
| `Show low-confidence detections` | project-analyzer | v18 — expand Phase 8.6 to include LOW-confidence framework/directory detections |
| `Apply all A` / `Apply all B` / `Ignore all` | project-analyzer (in-gate) | v18 batch operations during Phase 8.6 gate interaction |

Parsing rule: **most-specific-match-first**. See `project-analyzer.md § Rescan Command Router` for full spec including cascade/dependency logic and preservation rules.

## Flow (Step 0 + 5 story steps, gated)

**Step 0 (one-time or on-demand):** project-analyzer → produces/refreshes `contexts/project-map.md`
**Story flow:** orchestrator → explorer → surgeon → ac-e2e-check (optional) → review → ship (optional)

Consolidated: Architect merged into Orchestrator. Scout merged into Explorer.

## Gate template (ALL agents use this pattern)

Every agent gate MUST follow these rules:

**Rule 1: Show ONLY the immediate next action.** NEVER show future steps, future agents, or a roadmap. The user should only see what they need to do RIGHT NOW.

**Rule 2: Use a highlighted blockquote + Cursor deeplink for the next-agent handoff.** This grabs attention in Cursor AND lets Cursor users click through to a fresh chat (verified: new chat tab, not current-chat append) with the trigger pre-populated:

```
> **👉 Next action:** `{copy-paste command}` &nbsp; [▶ Open in new chat](cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/prompt?text={URL-encoded command})
```

- Cursor users: click the `▶` link → Cursor opens a new chat tab with the command pre-filled → hit Enter. Each agent runs in its own fresh conversation, so the prior agent's tool-call outputs don't bloat the next agent's context (prompt caching on stable files like `pipeline.yaml` splits, `project-map.md`, and skills stays efficient). Matches the "handoff via files, not chat history" rule below.
- Claude Code users: the deeplink renders as harmless markdown text; copy the `{copy-paste command}` and paste into a new conversation. (A separate Stop hook, if installed via the pipeline installer, copies the command to clipboard + shows a desktop notification — but the manual new-chat step is still required because Claude Code has no equivalent URL scheme.)

**URL-encoding rule** — replace these characters in the command before inserting into the `?text=` parameter:
`@` → `%40`, space → `%20`, `#` → `%23`, `&` → `%26`, `?` → `%3F`. Letters, digits, `.`, `-`, `_` are safe as-is.

**Deeplinks are ONLY for inter-agent handoffs** (the primary "next action" that invokes a NEW agent in a fresh chat). Gate options that CONTINUE the current conversation — `Amend:`, `Revise:`, `Go`, `Cancel`, `Show details`, `continue`, `retry` — do NOT get deeplinks; they act on the active chat.

**Rule 3: Provide example commands the user can copy.** Don't make the user guess the format — show the exact text they should type.

**Rule 4: List options as simple choices.** Each option should be a short command the user can copy.

**Gate format (ALL agents):**

```
## [Step X/5] {agent_name} — {phase/status} DONE

**Summary:**
- {key metric 1}
- {key metric 2}
- {key metric 3}

> **👉 Next action:** Pick one of the following:
> - `{primary next-agent action}` &nbsp; [▶ Open in new chat](cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/prompt?text={URL-encoded}) — {1-sentence description}
> - `{alternative action}` — {1-sentence description}       _(no deeplink — stays in current chat)_
> - `{show details action}` — {1-sentence description}      _(no deeplink — stays in current chat)_
> - `{modify action}` — {1-sentence description}            _(no deeplink — stays in current chat)_
```

