### Invocation modes (pipeline vs standalone)

Each agent supports two invocation modes:

- **Pipeline mode** — ticket-driven, full chain (Orchestrator → Explorer → Surgeon → Review → Ship, with optional AC-E2E-Check and Amender gate subagent). Produces full artifacts: LLD, test plan, manifest, review, PR. Inputs come from JIRA ticket + upstream agent outputs.
- **Standalone mode** — ad-hoc, single-agent. User provides context inline. Produces scoped artifacts only. No LLD, no PR. Good for research, one-off fixes, spot-checks.

Each agent's `detect_invocation_mode (0)` step (first pre-flight step) parses the trigger text to pick the mode. Pipeline mode runs the full flow; standalone mode skips prerequisite checks and runs a reduced flow.

**Standalone-mode support matrix:**

| Agent | Standalone | Standalone trigger pattern |
|-------|------------|---------------------------|
| orchestrator | N/A | (it's the start of the pipeline) |
| explorer | ✅ | `Research: <q>` or `Explore: <spec>` |
| explorer-bug | ❌ | No standalone — use `Research:` instead |
| surgeon | ✅ | `Apply: <spec>` (capped: ≤5 files, ≤150 lines) |
| review | ✅ | `Review changes` (compact — no blast radius / tests / AC compliance) |
| ac-e2e-check | ✅ | `Demo <URL>` or `Verify: <scenario>` |
| ship | ❌ | No standalone — safety rail, requires Review verdict |

**Standalone output convention:**

Standalone outputs live under `contexts/standalone/` with a `standalone-` filename prefix and timestamp:

```
contexts/
├── <epic>/                                       # pipeline artifacts per ticket
│   └── <TICKET>.md, <TICKET>-lld.md, ...
└── standalone/                                   # ad-hoc artifacts
    ├── standalone-exploration-{timestamp}.md
    ├── standalone-manifest-{timestamp}.md
    ├── standalone-review-{timestamp}.md
    └── standalone-demo-{timestamp}.md
```

Procedure B's glob (`contexts/**/{TICKET}.md`) ignores these because they don't match the `<TICKET>.md` pattern — standalone and pipeline artifacts never cross-contaminate.

### LLD document structure (split across three files)

```
$CONTEXTS_FILE  (main — small, human entry point)
  ─ Metadata (ticket, mode, base_branch, epic)
  ─ Requirement Summary         ← Explorer + Surgeon read for intent
  ─ Enriched AC Registry        ← Review + AC-E2E-Check read for coverage
  ─ Cross-Reference Findings
  ─ Companion Files             ← index: paths to $LLD_FILE + $TESTPLAN_FILE

$LLD_FILE       (design + implementation)
  # PART 1 — LLD Design          ← Review reads for compliance; Surgeon for WHY
  # PART 2 — LLD Tasks           ← Explorer + Surgeon read for implementation;
                                    AC-E2E-Check appends gap tasks

$TESTPLAN_FILE  (test plan + test tasks)
  # PART 3 — Test Plan           ← Review validates, Ship includes in PR
  # PART 4 — Test Tasks          ← Explorer + Surgeon read for test code
```

Bug mode: same three-file shape. Explorer-bug writes hypotheses to `$TESTPLAN_FILE` PART 3 (repurposed as "Root Cause Hypotheses" in bug mode) and fix tasks to `$LLD_FILE` PART 2 + regression tasks to `$TESTPLAN_FILE` PART 4.

## Build report contract (Surgeon ↔ Review ↔ Ship)

Every build invocation that goes through `builds.runner` (in pipeline config) writes a **structured build report file** alongside the raw log. Reports are the mechanism by which Review avoids re-running Surgeon's clean build, and by which Ship attaches build verdicts to the PR body — without any agent having to carry build output in its conversation context.

**Report schema** (YAML front-matter only — no prose body):

```yaml
---
agent: surgeon            # which agent ran the build
phase: final_build_check  # agent-internal step/task id (e.g. "review_gate", "T5", "pre_verify_build")
command: 'ant clean build'
status: 0                 # process exit code
verdict: PASS             # PASS | FAIL (derived from status)
started_at: 2026-04-23T14:32:10Z
ended_at:   2026-04-23T14:34:17Z
duration_s: 127
log_path: /tmp/pipeline-build.log
log_lines: 1847
tail_30: |
  [last 30 lines of stdout+stderr, indented 2 spaces so YAML accepts them as a literal block]
---
```

**Who writes what:**

| Report file | Writer | When |
|---|---|---|
| `$SURGEON_BUILD_REPORT` | Surgeon | After `final_build_check` (Step 4). Always written if a build ran. |
| `$REVIEW_BUILD_REPORT` | Review | After `full_clean_build` (Step 1a) — **only if Review actually ran a build** (skipped when Surgeon's report is fresh; see freshness check below). |
| `$DEMO_BUILD_REPORT` | AC-E2E-Check | After `pre_verify_build` (Step 1c) — only if `demo.pre_verify.run_build: true` (not the default). |

**Freshness check (Review's skip-if-fresh logic):**

Review's Step 1a runs this check BEFORE executing `builds.review_gate`:

```
IF $SURGEON_BUILD_REPORT exists:
    READ it → parse front-matter → extract .started_at and .verdict
    manifest_mtime = max(mtime of each file path listed in $MANIFEST_FILE)
    IF .verdict == PASS AND .started_at > manifest_mtime:
        echo "→ Surgeon's build report is fresh (verdict=PASS, started_at=...) — reusing verdict, skipping review_gate"
        copy .verdict / .command / .duration_s into $REVIEW_FILE under "Build Gate" heading
        DO NOT write $REVIEW_BUILD_REPORT (Surgeon's is the authority)
        proceed to Step 1b (unit_test_suite)
    ELSE:
        run review_gate normally → write $REVIEW_BUILD_REPORT
ELSE:
    run review_gate normally → write $REVIEW_BUILD_REPORT
```

`manifest_mtime` uses the Surgeon-declared file list rather than `git diff` to avoid false invalidation from Review's own in-flight fixes.

**Reading build reports (for downstream agents):**

- **On PASS**: downstream agents should read front-matter fields only (status/verdict/duration_s/command). Do NOT read `tail_30` — it's there for post-mortem, not happy-path context.
- **On FAIL**: read the full report including `tail_30`. If `tail_30` is insufficient, use the `Read` tool to open `log_path` directly (may not exist on fresh chats — the log file is machine-local and ephemeral; the report's `tail_30` is the durable record).

**Invariants:**

- A report file is written ATOMICALLY — the runner template writes to `{report}.tmp` first in future iterations; for now, a partial report on an interrupted build is tolerable because the agent will re-run the wrapped command.
- Report files are committed to git along with other `contexts/{TICKET}-*.md` artifacts by Ship — they become part of the permanent record of the story.
- No agent should delete an existing report; overwriting (on re-run) is fine.

## Tool Usage Tracking (ALL agents — single ledger per pipeline run)

Every agent in the pipeline appends a structured block to `$TOOL_USAGE_FILE` at the **end of its run**, just before rendering the final `## [Step N/5] {agent} — DONE` gate. The ledger is the single audit artifact for "where did this pipeline run actually spend its time and tokens?"

### File location

| Run mode | Path |
|---|---|
| Single-story (story / bug) | `$CONTEXT_DIR + {TICKET}-tool-usage.md` |
| Bundle | `$BUNDLE_CONTEXT_DIR + {BUNDLE_ID}-tool-usage.md` |
| Standalone | `contexts/standalone/standalone-{agent}-tool-usage-{timestamp}.md` (per agent run; not aggregated) |

The variable `$TOOL_USAGE_FILE` resolves to the appropriate path at pre-flight (path-resolution table extends Procedure A/B/C). Created on first agent's append; subsequent agents append to the same file.

### When to write

At the **last step of the agent's run, immediately before the final gate is rendered**. Write happens regardless of verdict (PASS or FAIL or HALT) so failed runs are still audited. If an agent crashes before reaching the final step, the prior agents' blocks remain on disk — this is acceptable.

For checkpointed agents (bundle-explorer, bundle-surgeon, bundle-review): write a **partial block at each checkpoint** (with `## explorer (checkpoint at T<N>)` heading) and a **final block** at end-of-stage. Each checkpoint's block is independently parseable.

### Schema (markdown — block per agent run)

The first agent to run creates the file with the YAML frontmatter. Subsequent agents append their block.

```markdown
---
ticket: PROJ-1234           # OR bundle_id: epic-42-bundle-a3f2
mode: story                 # story | bug | bundle | standalone
created_at: 2026-05-06T10:00:00Z
---

# Tool Usage Ledger

## {agent_name}{run_marker}
- Started:  {ISO8601}
- Ended:    {ISO8601}
- Duration: {seconds}s
- Mode:     {pipeline | standalone}{ · sub_mode if any}
- Verdict:  {DONE | HALT(reason) | FAIL(reason)}

